Read-only code check, no rebuild. Your guess (old exe still running) fits, and I can't prove it from here.

1) Paths: ocr.log, crash.log, ocr_child_stderr.log and settings.json all sit in the same folder, %APPDATA%\LitePDF (that is Roaming\LitePDF, as he checked). crash.log is created only when a panic or Windows exception happens, so no crash.log is not proof the old exe ran. But the new build writes new-format ocr.log lines on every OCR run: "starting worker process...", "stage: ...", "memory (worker): ...". If his ocr.log has none of those, the new exe did not run OCR. One caveat: a hard kill (TDR driver reset, Task Manager, out-of-memory kill) bypasses both the panic hook and the exception filter. In that case ocr.log would still show new-format stage lines up to the last stage, and the parent would log "WORKER ENDED EARLY" if only the worker died. If the whole app vanished with new-format lines, that is the useful clue.

2) Installer: default folder is %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\LitePDF, and if an install already exists it reads the registry InstallLocation and reuses that folder, so it upgrades in place (renames the old litepdf.exe to .old, writes the new one, deletes .old). One Start Menu and one Desktop shortcut, both overwritten. Ways he can still run the old build: (a) LitePDF was still open during install, so the running process stays old until closed and reopened; (b) he launches an exe from somewhere else (a copy from the earlier zip/Downloads, or a pinned/taskbar shortcut or "Open with" pointing at another path); (c) the installer's folder page was changed to a different folder, giving two installs. (The silent installer isn't used by him.)

